{"task_id": "HumanEval/20", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\ndef find_closest_elements(numbers: List[float]) -> Tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\" From a supplied list of numbers, select and return two that are the closest to each\n    other in order (smaller number, larger number).\n    \"\"\"\n    numbers.sort()\n    min_diff = float('inf')\n    closest_pair = (numbers[0], numbers[1])\n\n    for i in range(len(numbers) - 1):\n        diff = numbers[i+1] - numbers[i]\n        if diff < min_diff:\n            min_diff = diff\n            closest_pair = (numbers[i], numbers[i+1])\n\n    return closest_pair\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/20", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\ndef find_closest_elements(numbers: List[float]) -> Tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"Finds two numbers in a list that are closest in magnitude.\n    Returns them as a tuple (smaller_value, larger_value).\"\"\"\n\n    # Sort the list to find the globally closest pair by checking adjacent elements.\n    # This approach satisfies the provided doctests.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # Initialize minimum difference and the pair found so far.\n    min_diff = float('inf')\n    closest_pair = (0.0, 0.0)\n\n    # Iterate through adjacent elements in the sorted list to find the minimum difference.\n    # The elements 'a' and 'b' are already sorted, so a <= b.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        a = sorted_numbers[i]\n        b = sorted_numbers[i+1]\n        current_diff = abs(a - b)\n\n        if current_diff < min_diff:\n            min_diff = current_diff\n            closest_pair = (a, b)\n\n    return closest_pair\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/21", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef rescale_to_unit(numbers: List[float]) -> List[float]:\n    \"\"\" Given list of numbers (of at least two elements), apply a linear transform to that list,\n    such that the smallest number will become 0 and the largest will become 1\n    \"\"\"\n    min_val = min(numbers)\n    max_val = max(numbers)\n\n    # If all numbers are identical, map them all to 0.0.\n    if min_val == max_val:\n        return [0.0] * len(numbers)\n\n    rescaled_numbers = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        rescaled_num = (num - min_val) / (max_val - min_val)\n        rescaled_numbers.append(rescaled_num)\n\n    return rescaled_numbers\n```"}
